/P 289 0 R
/E (Somewhere between the first word and the final page, a book passes through many hands. What we don't see is everything that happens along the way.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (A book does not simply appear on a shelf. It begins as an idea waiting to take shape. Words are written, ideas are explored, and pages slowly begin to come together. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
488 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (But a story on paper is only the beginning. It needs to be read, questioned, and looked at from different perspectives before it can become what it is meant to be.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
489 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (A story is rarely perfect in its first form. Some words change, some ideas move, some pages disappear, and sometimes a small suggestion can make a big difference. With every change, the story becomes a little clearer and a little stronger. Slowly, what was once a draft starts becoming a book we can imagine holding in our hands.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (Then the words meet pictures. A character that lived only in our imagination gets a face. A place gets its colours. A moment we had only read about suddenly becomes something we can see. Especially in a children's book, )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (the pictures become a part of the story too, giving the reader another way to enter its world. Then comes the moment when all these pieces need to come together. Words, pictures, colours, and pages are brought into one place, each making room for the other. A picture may need more space, a sentence may need to move, or a page may need to be rearranged. Slowly, the separate pieces begin to feel like one complete book.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
